\section{Statuses}{

A card's \code{status} is the id of a status. Five are built in: \code{"done"}, \code{"running"}, \code{"waiting"}, \code{"failed"} and
\code{"skipped"}. The \code{statuses} argument takes two forms:
\itemize{
\item A named character vector, such as \code{c(done = "Pass", failed = "Not passed")}, gives your word for a built-in status
(the short form). The names are built-in ids; any other name is dropped with a warning.
\item A data frame has one row per status, with the columns \verb{id*}, \code{label}, \code{tone} and \code{icon} (the name of one of the
engine's icons). A row whose \code{id} is a built-in id restyles that status (its label, colour and icon), and does not
add a sixth. A row with any other id adds a status of your own, such as \code{"blocked"}.
}

\code{nodes$status} (and the \code{status} of \code{\link[=add_steps]{add_steps()}}'s \code{changes}) may then use any id from \code{statuses}; any other id is
an error of class \code{stagemap_error_data} that names it, lists the ids allowed and says to add it to \code{statuses}. An id
given twice in a \code{statuses} data frame is an error too. A \code{tone} is checked against the tones listed under
"Columns", and the error names the value and lists the ones allowed. Give \code{statuses} in one form per call.
}

\section{Scrolling and moving}{

Stage headings and lane names stay in view while the reader scrolls the map, but only when the map sits in a box
that scrolls itself; a page that scrolls as a whole does not keep them in view. In the layout that stacks stage by
stage (a box under 500 pixels wide), only the stage headings stay in view.

When a step of \code{\link[=add_steps]{add_steps()}} moves a card to another stage, the card glides there, and a card that appears fades
in. The glide follows the reader's reduced-motion setting. \code{motion = FALSE} turns it off.
}

